the fall of constantinople in 1453 is  considered by many to be one of the most  important events in human history  to some modern historians it marks the  end of the medieval period  and to others it merely marks the final  end of the long drawn-out decline of the  roman empire and the beginnings of  ottoman control over this region  but how did the contemporary people of  europe react to this event  so after the city's fault amendment you  can now call me the conqueror ii  the first europeans to hear about the  news outside of the city itself were  venetian and genoese traders  and they immediately returned to their  respective homes and spread the news  to which the response in those cities  was absolute terror  not because of constantinople's  importance historically or religiously  but because their trade had just been  impacted and they really liked money  news spread further and it wasn't long  before the city of rome and the papacy  knew of constantinople's demise  the sitting pope nicholas v was  absolutely terrified and he immediately  put out the call for a crusade to retake  the city  now he had this reaction for two main  reasons the first was that  constantinople's capture now meant that  the ottomans could turn their full  attention to the north the second was  that rome was now the last of the five  major christian cities not under the  control of non-christians and as a  result those that lived there assumed  that they would be next  there was also some salt in the wound  since roman constantinople were making  great strides in fixing their previous  schism  so what about the rest of europe and its  secular rulers well in france the king  was obviously disheartened by the news  but much like his english counterpart he  was busy fighting a war and so was a tad  preoccupied  also leaving the kingdom that you ruled  for several years meant that it was open  to being usurped or perhaps re-invaded  by those you just defeated  as for the holy roman empire their  relationship with the byzantine empire  was a tad more complicated whilst the  leadership there saw the city of  constantinople as a bastion of  christianity it was populated by the  wrong sort of christians  whom everyone had spent the last 400  years condemning his heretics and so  frankly there was no love lost  also no one could argue about who was  the true successor to the roman empire  anymore  all of this said the holy roman empire  now knew that the ottomans would be  pushing north  the emperor at this time frederick iii  was much more concerned with internal  problems and keeping his domains in line  and so he had little to spare for any  crusade  as for the other major catholic nations  of europe their responses are basically  non-existent meaning that in all  likelihood it wasn't that much of a big  deal to them or they simply had more  pressing issues  but what about europe's leading  non-catholic nation the orthodox  principality of moscow well its prince  vasily  ii had previously had a falling out with  the leadership of constantinople due to  its aforementioned making up with the  pope he had previously declared the  moscow orthodox church to be independent  of the byzantine one and with  constantinople now gone  his was now the leading branch and so he  was just fine with it and as vasili's  successors would go on to do  moscow could now claim to be the third  rome and the latest in the succession of  the roman empire of antiquity  and so from this we can see that for the  most part europe's reaction barring that  of the italian states was fairly muted  this was mostly because the fool of a  city of heretics even a prestigious one  like constantinople had little immediate  effect on them  and as for the pope's crusade well  nicholas did soon try to force the hands  of europe's secular leadership but  before he could begin assembling a  christian response he caught a small  case of death  meaning that no attempt to retake the  city was ever made  i hope you enjoyed this episode and  thank you for watching with a special  thanks to my patrons  james bissonette kelly moneymaker the  pastry section marvin casou  rob waterhouse danny maloney mo aaron  the white james castaneda  jordan longley marcus azner gerry  lambdon gustav swann rasheed ali  colin carson copper tone izzy david  silverman spinning three plates  winston k wood spencer lightfoot maggie  paxkowski  matthew shipley anthony beckett  fortunate calf robert wetzel lexy  schwinn and skye chappelle
 

